# SETUID(2)

## NAME

setuid, setgid - set user and group ID

## SYNOPSIS

```c
int setuid(int uid);

int setgid(int gid);
```

## DESCRIPTION

The user ID (group ID) of the current process is set to the argument. Both the effective and
the real ID are set. These calls are only permitted to the super-user or if the argument is the
real ID.

## SEE ALSO

getuid(2)

## DIAGNOSTICS

Zero is returned if the user (group) ID is set; -1 is returned otherwise.

## ASSEMBLER

**Note:** v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here - a decimal call number and a `sys name; arg ...`
line. Neither is how a call is made on this machine. The gate is one extracode - **$77
SYS_setuid, SYS_setgid** - and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it
reads them: 1..n-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the
error number in r14.

**The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.** The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is
the only place a syscall number appears on the user side. See intro(2) for the whole contract.
